data.content:
## Migration Analysis

**3 patterns found** in your `EventProcessor`:

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|----------|---------|-----------------|
| `Stop()` → `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `Stop()` → `_thread.Abort()` | Fallback kill | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `RunLoop()` → `while(true)` + `WaitOne()` | Cancellable work loop | No way to exit cooperatively |

Also: `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue<T>` + `lock` is a hand-rolled producer/consumer — .NET 4+ has `BlockingCollection<T>` and `Channel<T>` for this.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _runTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — token was cancelled
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask EnqueueAsync(string item)
    {
        await _channel.Writer.WriteAsync(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();   // Signal no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();               // Unblock if waiting for items
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### What changed and why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<string>` | Thread-safe producer/consumer built-in; no manual synchronization |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative cancellation — `ReadAllAsync` checks the token |
| `_thread.Abort()` | `_channel.Writer.Complete()` | Signals end-of-stream; loop exits naturally |
| `_thread.Join(5000)` | `await _runTask` | Async wait for clean completion |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` (implicit) | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | Modern cancellation exception |

---

## Key Pitfalls

**1. `Handle()` must also cooperate.** If `Handle` does long-running work, pass the token into it — cancellation only works at checkpoints:
```csharp
private void Handle(string item, CancellationToken token)
{
    // Check between expensive steps
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
}
```

**2. Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It's `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. Always dispose in `StopAsync`.

**3. `WaitOne()` can't be interrupted by a token.** Your original `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` would need `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _newEvent, token.WaitHandle })` if you kept the manual approach. The `Channel` solution avoids this entirely.

**4. Race condition in the original code.** If multiple items are enqueued before `RunLoop` processes one, `Reset()` clears the signal and subsequent items are lost. `Channel` fixes this — it's a proper queue with built-in signaling.

**5. Synchronous callers.** If you can't make callers async, provide sync wrappers:
```csharp
public void Enqueue(string item) =>
    _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item); // non-blocking for unbounded

public void Stop()
{
    _channel.Writer.Complete();
    _cts?.Cancel();
    _runTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    _cts?.Dispose();
}
```

**6. Don't substitute `Thread.Interrupt` for `Thread.Abort`.** Both are obsolete/broken in modern .NET. Always use `CancellationToken`.
